[2025-12-05 17:14:06] [AGENT_RESPONSE]
Response: content='When designing a fire alarm system, you should adhere to the following key
standards and codes, depending on your location:\n\n### **In Canada:**\n1. **CAN/ULC-S524** – This
is the primary standard for fire alarm system design and installation in Canada. It covers:\n   -
Detector placement and spacing (e.g., smoke detectors based on room area and ceiling height).\n   -
Layout and installation of manual alarm stations, sounders, and strobes.\n   - Power supply
requirements, including battery capacity and backup duration.\n   - Sound pressure levels for
audible devices (e.g., alarm bells) and visual alarms (strobe lights).\n   - System wiring,
including wire selection and installation methods.\n\n2. **CAN/ULC-S536** – The annual inspection
standard for fire alarm systems.\n3. **CAN/ULC-S537** – Specifies testing and verification
procedures for new installations or renovations.\n\n### **In the United States:**\n1. **NFPA 72
(National Fire Alarm and Signaling Code)** – The primary standard governing fire alarm system
design, installation, and maintenance. It includes:\n   - Requirements for detector types,
placement, and coverage.\n   - Sound pressure levels for audible alarms (typically 75 dB minimum at
the listener’s location).\n   - Visual alarm requirements (e.g., strobe intensity and coverage).\n
- System integration with fire departments (e.g., automatic notification).\n   - Power supply and
backup battery requirements (e.g., 24-hour battery backup).\n\n2. **NEC (National Electrical Code,
NFPA 70)** – Governs electrical wiring and installation methods for fire alarm systems, including:\n
- Wire types and conduit requirements.\n   - Separation from other electrical circuits.\n   -
Grounding and bonding.\n\n### **General Design Considerations:**\n- **Building Codes** – Always
check local building codes, as they determine whether a fire alarm system is required and may
specify additional requirements based on building type (e.g., high-rise, healthcare, schools).\n-
**Acoustic and Visual Standards** – Ensure alarms meet minimum decibel levels (e.g., 75 dB) and
strobe visibility requirements (e.g., 100 cd/m² for visual alarms).\n- **Power Supply** – Design for
reliable power with sufficient battery backup (typically 24 hours) and proper charging circuits.\n-
**Integration** – Consider integration with fire suppression systems, emergency lighting, and public
address systems.\n\n### **Summary:**\n- **Canada:** Follow **CAN/ULC-S524** as the core design
standard.\n- **USA:** Follow **NFPA 72** and **NEC** for comprehensive design and installation
compliance.\n\nAlways verify requirements with local authorities having jurisdiction (AHJs) and
consult the latest editions of these standards, as they are regularly updated.' additional_kwargs={}
